Approach 1: Find Maximum First, Then Find Second Maximum
Idea
Traverse the array once to find the maximum element and its index.
Traverse again to find the largest element excluding the maximum's index.
Return (max - 1) * (secondMax - 1).
Code
class Solution {
    public int maxProduct(int[] nums) {
        int max = 0, secondMax = 0;
        int maxIndex = -1;

        // Find maximum element and its index
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] > max) {
                max = nums[i];
                maxIndex = i;
            }
        }

        // Find second maximum excluding maxIndex
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (i != maxIndex && nums[i] > secondMax) {
                secondMax = nums[i];
            }
        }

        return (max - 1) * (secondMax - 1);
    }
}
Time Complexity
O(n) + O(n) = O(n)
Space Complexity
O(1)

Approach 2: Single Pass (Optimal)
Idea
Maintain two variables:
max1 → largest element
max2 → second largest element
Update both in a single traversal.
Code
class Solution {
    public int maxProduct(int[] nums) {
        int max1 = 0;
        int max2 = 0;

        for (int num : nums) {
            if (num > max1) {
                max2 = max1;
                max1 = num;
            } else if (num > max2) {
                max2 = num;
            }
        }

        return (max1 - 1) * (max2 - 1);
    }
}
Time Complexity
O(n)
Space Complexity
O(1)

Approach 3: Sorting
Idea
Sort the array.
The last two elements are the largest.
Compute the required product.
Code
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int maxProduct(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        return (nums[nums.length - 1] - 1) * (nums[nums.length - 2] - 1);
    }
}
Time Complexity
O(n log n)
Space Complexity
O(log n) (sorting stack space for Java's Dual-Pivot Quicksort on primitive arrays)

Complexity Comparison

| Approach                  | Time           | Space        |
| ------------------------- | -------------- | ------------ |
| Find Max, Then Second Max | **O(n)**       | **O(1)**     |
| Single Pass (Optimal)     | **O(n)**       | **O(1)**     |
| Sorting                   | **O(n log n)** | **O(log n)** |
